    "result_text": "Both tests pass and the build is green.\n\nHere's what I changed to implement the redesigned identity key while keeping structural impact low:\n\n**1. New unit `OwnerSoundex.java`** \u2014 a small, self-contained class computing standard 4-char Soundex. Isolating it here means the surrounding-complexity cost (`WMC_other`) for the phonetic logic is ~0, rather than inflating a busy class.\n\n**2. `OwnerIdentityKey.of(...)`** \u2014 one line changed:\n```java\nreturn sha256Hex(telephone(telephone) + '|' + email(email) + '|' + OwnerSoundex.soundex(lastName));\n```\nNow `identityKey` = SHA-256 hex over `normalizedTelephone|lowerEmail|soundex(lastName)` (a 64-char hex string). The `householdId` helper is untouched \u2014 still used for the separate response field \u2014 so the mapper needed no change.\n\n**3. `OwnerUniqueIdentityAdvice.afterBodyRead`** \u2014 the 409 duplicate check now compares the full `OwnerIdentityKey.of(...)` instead of a hand-built `telephone|email` string, so duplicate detection is driven by the single identity key (still skipping deleted owners; the disposable-email blocklist advice still runs first at the same precedence).\n\n**4. `OwnerPossibleDuplicateAdvice`** \u2014 replaced the always-false stub with a soft-match: it flags the returned owner as a possible duplicate of the earliest non-deleted earlier owner sharing `soundex(lastName)` + postcode but with a *different* identity key. This is exactly the new case where two owners share last name and postcode but differ in telephone \u2014 no longer a hard 409, now a soft match.\n\nThe old household-derived 409 path is gone since duplicate detection is now the single identity key. Each edited method stayed simple (small CC, few changed lines), the phonetic complexity lives in its own low-context unit, and no large class grew.",
